# Codex workflow reference

## Session defaults (last verified 2026-09-06)

- Plain `codex` starts with `{{ .agents.codex.defaults.model }}` at {{ .agents.codex.defaults.model_reasoning_effort }} reasoning effort;
  plan mode is pinned to {{ .agents.codex.defaults.plan_mode_reasoning_effort }}.
- `/model` changes the model or reasoning effort for the current session only; a new
  session returns to the managed defaults.
- No launch profiles are configured. Start with plain `codex` and make any temporary
  session changes in the TUI.
- Plan mode is selected per session; this setup does not persist a default mode
  override.
- `/fast` is a separate speed toggle, not a model or reasoning-effort profile.

## Limits (last verified 2026-09-10; revisit Sol pricing 2026-11-21)

- Subscription metering is token-based credits (since 2026-04): one shared
  credit pool across CLI, IDE extension, cloud tasks, and other ChatGPT agent
  surfaces, drawn against a weekly window plus a rolling 5-hour window — treat
  both as potentially binding; check `/status`.
- Standard credits per million input/cached/output tokens: Astra 250/25/1250,
  Sol 100/10/500, Terra 50/5/300, Luna 5/0.5/30. These are credit rates, not API
  dollar charges or fixed Pro message limits. Sol's promotional pricing lasts
  at least through 2026-11-21. [Official rate card](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/pricing).
- Compare successful-task cost including reasoning, cached context, retries and
  delegation overhead. A larger model can use fewer tokens without being cheaper.
  Astra Standard costs 2.5× Sol at equivalent token usage; Astra Fast is a further
  2.5× Standard. Keep bounded work on the lower tiers and Fast off unless needed.
- Reasoning effort scales burn directly: drop effort per session via `/model`
  for routine work instead of changing the managed default.
- Context window is 272,000 input tokens on the ChatGPT-subscription route, and
  the harness inherits it from the model catalog — nothing here pins it. Do not
  raise `model_context_window` above it: Codex clamps the value to the catalog's
  `max_context_window` and then to ~95%, so a larger number is cosmetic
  (`/status` shows it until the first message, then the real cap) and it
  miscalibrates auto-compaction, which is sized off the configured window. The
  1,050,000 window the model pages advertise is real but API-key only, and input
  above 272,000 bills the whole request at 2x input / 1.5x output. This ceiling
  moved 272K -> 372K -> 872K -> 272K across 2026 without notice, so read
  `/status` rather than trusting any number written down — including this one.
  (Verified 2026-09-10 against the pinned SDK catalog; revisit at the next Codex
  CLI catalog bump, or if `/status` reports anything but 272K.)
- Attached MCP servers reload their full tool schemas every turn — an accepted
  cost here; revisit the server list if the weekly limit starts binding.

## Subagents (last verified 2026-07-31; expiry fired 2026-09-08 and is still open — re-verify the pin behaviour from a Codex session. The 2026-09-09 Claude-side audit confirmed it cannot close this: the permission gate denied `~/.codex/sessions` on two attempts, so the store stays unreadable from that side. Next trigger: the first Codex session after a Codex CLI update.)

- Named specialists under `~/.codex/agents` stay pinned to their configured model tier,
  even after `/model` changes the parent session.
- Known upstream regression (openai/codex#32587, MultiAgent V2; verified still
  open 2026-09-07, last upstream activity 2026-07-12): spawned subagents can silently inherit the parent's
  model/effort instead of their pinned config. After Codex CLI updates, verify
  a specialist actually ran its pin via `/status` or the session log before
  trusting tier economics.
- `implementer` (added 2026-08-28): mid-tier (Terra) executor for
  spec-complete mechanical write work — the weighted credit rates make it far
  cheaper than inline edits in a grown session, but #32587 above can silently
  erase exactly that economics, so the pin check applies doubly here.
- OpenAI's own Codex guidance routes read-heavy exploration subagents to Terra;
  the small tier here stays on Luna (bounded extraction-shaped presets, far
  cheaper) — revisit if explorer output quality disappoints.

## Frontier escalation (last verified 2026-09-06)

- The shared OpenAI frontier tier is `{{ .subagent_tiers.codex.frontier }}`;
  existing specialist pins and the main-session default are unchanged.
- Use it for an explicit escalation or consequential architecture/review task,
  preferably with a bounded handoff in a fresh session. Do not promote every role.
- Availability depends on the account and installed harness. A model catalog
  entry is not proof of account access; never substitute another provider or paid
  API routing when subscription access is unavailable.
